<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Omar Khayyam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1048">1048</a>–1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Omar Khayyam was a Persian mathematician, astronomer and poet of the eleventh and early twelfth centuries. He worked under the patronage of the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a> and took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalali-calendar">reforming the solar calendar</a> with great astronomical precision. He is best known internationally for the quatrains, or rubaiyat, attributed to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam.jpg" alt="Omar Khayyam" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alireza Javaheri · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hakim_Omar_Khayam_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poet and mathematician <b>Omar Khayyam</b> worked under Seljuk patronage on the Jalali solar calendar of 1079, more accurate than the Julian calendar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Omar Khayyam</b>, also a great mathematician and astronomer, wrote quatrains on the brevity of life; Nizami wrote romantic epics such as Layla and Majnun and Khosrow and Shirin; Attar wrote the Conference of the Birds, an allegory of the mystical path; Saadi wrote the Gulistan, “The Rose Garden”,…</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.21,58.8&amp;year=1101" aria-label="Nishapur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6030 857 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Omar Khayyam"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6480 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6480 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.21,58.8&amp;year=1101">What was here in 1101? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A Book of Verses underneath the Bough, A Jug of Wine, a Loaf of Bread—and Thou Beside me singing in the Wilderness— Oh, Wilderness were Paradise enow!&#8221;</p><footer>Rubaiyat, quatrain 12 (5th edition) &#183; tr. Edward FitzGerald, 1889 &#183; FitzGerald's free English version of quatrains ascribed to him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/246/pg246-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Moving Finger writes; and, having writ, Moves on: nor all your Piety nor Wit Shall lure it back to cancel half a Line, Nor all your Tears wash out a Word of it.&#8221;</p><footer>Rubaiyat, quatrain 71 (5th edition) &#183; tr. Edward FitzGerald, 1889 &#183; FitzGerald's free English version of quatrains ascribed to him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/246/pg246-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ah Love! could you and I with Him conspire To grasp this sorry Scheme of Things entire, Would not we shatter it to bits—and then Re-mold it nearer to the Heart's Desire!&#8221;</p><footer>Rubaiyat, quatrain 99 (5th edition) &#183; tr. Edward FitzGerald, 1889 &#183; FitzGerald's free English version of quatrains ascribed to him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/246/pg246-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Worldly Hope men set their Hearts upon Turns Ashes—or it prospers; and anon, Like Snow upon the Desert's dusty Face Lighting a little Hour or two—is gone.&#8221;</p><footer>Rubaiyat, quatrain 14 (1st edition) &#183; tr. Edward FitzGerald, 1859 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/246/pg246-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Think, in this batter'd Caravanserai Whose Doorways are alternate Night and Day, How Sultan after Sultan with his Pomp Abode his Hour or two, and went his way.&#8221;</p><footer>Rubaiyat, quatrain 16 (1st edition) &#183; tr. Edward FitzGerald, 1859 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/246/pg246-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Myself when young did eagerly frequent Doctor and Saint, and heard great Argument About it and about: but evermore Came out by the same Door as in I went.&#8221;</p><footer>Rubaiyat, quatrain 27 (1st edition) &#183; tr. Edward FitzGerald, 1859 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/246/pg246-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
